 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-meaning"><h3>What is Qada Salah and when is it required?</h3><p>Qada refers to a prayer made up after its appointed time. In <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:684a">Sahih Muslim 684a</a>, the Prophet ﷺ describes prayer missed through sleep or forgetfulness and praying when remembered. Schools agree on this general obligation for those cases; broader discussions of deliberately omitted prayers and exclusions differ and require qualified guidance. A calculator cannot issue a fatwa based on a number of missed days.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-schools"><h3>Scholarly differences: deliberate omission, Witr and exemptions</h3><p>The four Sunni schools commonly address making up intentionally missed Fard prayers, but there are differing scholarly positions about the underlying duty and associated repentance. For a Hanafi explanation see <a href="https://seekersguidance.org/answers/living-religion/do-pass-missed-prayers-have-to-be-made-up/">SeekersGuidance's Qada discussion</a>. Witr is emphasised differently between the Hanafi school and other schools; the optional Witr checkbox is not a declaration that everyone owes it. Exemptions for menstruation and other circumstances also require their own treatment.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-estimate"><h3>How to estimate missed prayers without pretending certainty</h3><ol><li>Ask a qualified scholar which period and prayer types actually apply to your situation.</li><li>Choose an estimated number of days, approximate years or inclusive civil date range.</li><li>Exclude days only if your religious guidance says those prayers were not owed.</li><li>Review the default one-each-day Fajr through Isha counts and override individual types when needed.</li><li>Choose whether to include Witr under your school's guidance.</li><li>Track only genuinely performed make-up prayers and make a local JSON backup before resetting or moving devices.</li></ol><p>A year is estimated with an average civil-year length, not a guarantee that five prayers were owed every day of that period. Treat an uncertain estimate explicitly as uncertain.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-pace"><h3>Planning a manageable Qada pace</h3><p>The optional new pace control estimates the number of civil days needed to clear the <em>remaining calculated target</em> at a chosen number of make-up prayers per day. It is an organisational projection only, not a religious deadline, recommended number or permission to neglect current obligatory prayers. A change in completed counts updates the remaining projection automatically.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-example"><h3>Worked example: 30 review days and five prayers each</h3><div class="three-tool-example"><p>If a qualified assessment identifies thirty applicable days with one of each five Fard prayers to make up, the planning target is <strong>30 × 5 = 150 prayers</strong>. If ten have already been completed and you choose an illustrative pace of <strong>5 per civil day</strong>, the remaining 140 prayers would take <strong>28 planning days</strong>.</p><p>Those numbers are not a statement that anyone owes 150 prayers. Excluded days, Witr, individual target overrides, illness and historical uncertainty may change the figures; ask your school which obligations apply.</p></div></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-privacy"><h3>Your Qada plan and completed progress stay on your device</h3><p>The tool preserves the existing versioned local plan with day/year/range inputs, overrides and completed prayer counts. JSON backup and restoration are opt-in; backup files are validated and replacement requires confirmation. Clearing site storage or switching devices can delete saved progress. Unlike the Salah Tracker, the Qada plan does not infer missed obligations from empty daily checkboxes.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-not"><h3>Qada versus prayer-time reminders and the daily Salah tracker</h3><p>A prayer timetable tells you when today's prayer window begins; the <a href="/tools/prayer-times/">Prayer Times tool</a> provides calculated start times. The <a href="/tools/salah-tracker/">Salah Tracker</a> records voluntary checkmarks and does not create liabilities. The Qada calculator holds only an estimate of a religiously reviewed past target and your own progress. These three functions should not be conflated.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="qada-deep-questions"><h3>What to check before relying on a Qada estimate</h3><p>Verify the religious date at which prayer became obligatory for the person, any genuinely exempt periods, circumstances of missing the prayers, Witr requirements, and the school's approach to making them up. Also consider practical health and ability; a plan's arithmetic cannot determine someone's capacity. Ensure a realistic pace does not displace current obligations and consult an appropriate qualified teacher for complex cases.</p></section>
